From 453bffa858de7407e3a8e82a7c244a49d61172fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 16:52:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(equipment): four renditions, a Ken Burns rotator, and
 the symptom joins the machine (#34)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CODE ONLY, and it does not apply on its own. The 64 photographs are installed
by scripts/install-equipment-images.sh from the two zips already on main, and
the zips are then untracked by hand. A patch that base64-encodes 29 MB of JPEG
is 74 MB, and one that also deletes two zips is 37 MB more, because a binary
deletion carries the whole file. The order of operations is in
docs/DOMINATION_CHANGELOG.md under Stage 14. Run the install script BEFORE
npm run verify — the tests check the manifest against the disk and will
correctly fail without the files.

Two agents were handed one brief and each delivered a full pair, so there are
four renditions of all sixteen subjects rather than two. The variant system was
hard-coded to exactly two — an array of paths and a function that appended
"-2". It is now a path->count map, IMAGES_WITH_VARIANTS is derived from its
keys so no existing call site moved, and the manifest is checked against disk
in BOTH directions. The second direction is the one that matters: sixty-four
images could otherwise ship in the repository, cost their bytes in every clone,
and never once be served.

74 images -> 138. 37 subjects -> 53. Sixteen of them quartets.

PhotoRotator already existed and already paused on hover, off-screen, in a
hidden tab and under reduced motion. It was extended, not replaced — rewriting
it would have broken /about and /showroom and thrown away discipline that was
already right. Three additions: `seed` rotates the sequence so the first frame
is exactly pickVariant's answer (asserted, and it is what lets the server
render a correct still with the motion layered on top); `kenBurns` drifts 1.00
to 1.06 over eighteen seconds, opt-in because it is wrong on anything a reader
studies — a moisture-meter screen must hold still while it is read; `priority`
marks the first frame only, so the one-eager-image rule survives four stacked
images. Reduced motion is cancelled explicitly as well as by the global clamp,
because freezing correctly by accident is not the same as saying so.

The six DEFECT images are the ones worth having. Chatter, the perimeter halo,
cupping, hollow spots, lap lines, a newel that moves — each caused by a machine
that was missing or misused, and four of them now joined to the diagnosis page
for the same symptom. "What it looks like" was a paragraph asking the reader to
imagine something; it is a photograph now, linking to the reason. Nobody in
this market connects the symptom to the equipment.

The other four machine classes get no defect image and a test pins that list by
name: their failures are not visually checkable, and a near-enough picture is
the same error as inventing a price band for a job with no stated scale.

Pictures make the inventory rule easier to break, so the enforcement grew with
them. EQUIPMENT_IMAGE_DISCLOSURE and DEFECT_IMAGE_DISCLOSURE render under every
instance; a test fails the build if any surface shows one without it, and
asserts "not an asset list" and "not an inventory" alongside so they cannot
drift apart. Blocker #13 stays open — the renders make the section look
finished, only real photographs make it true, and none of them may go near the
Google Business Profile.

*.zip is gitignored and a hygiene test now fails on a tracked archive. Same
history as the three patch files, same reason.

387 tests pass (+25). 404 pages, unchanged — this stage adds depth, not surface.
Audit clean. 10 routes x 4 viewports, no overflow.

